<section xml:id="notes-week-10" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Week 10</title>

<introduction>
    <p>
        This is an outline of the topics we covered in week 10 of the course, from November 2 to November 6.
    </p>
</introduction>


<handout component="cur">
    <title>Monday 11/2</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Investigate finding zeros of polynomials.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Explore different methods of factoring polynomials.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Use polynomials to model real world scenarios.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Polynomials</title>

        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            Last time, we defined the basic terms surrounding polynomials. 
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            Last time, we defined the basic terms surrounding polynomials.  Formula, degree, leading term and end behavior.
        </p>


        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Zeros of Polynomials</title>
                    <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            One of the other most important things about polynomials is trying to find the zeros.
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            One of the other most important things about polynomials is trying to find the zeros. This is something that we did extensively for quadratics. What are zeros, what does it mean to find them?
        </p>
        </paragraphs>

    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider <m>f(x) = x^3 - 2x^2 - 5x + 6</m>. Determine if <m>x=2</m> and <m>x=1</m> are zeros of this polynomial?
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <p component="b" workspace="2in">
            One of the easiest way to find zeros of quadratics was using the principle of zero products.
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            One of the easiest way to find zeros of quadratics was using the principle of zero products. This meant factoring the polynomial.
        </p>

        <example workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the zeros of the function <m>f(x) = (x-1)^4(x+3)^2(x-5)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the zeros of the polynomial <m>f(x) = (x+2)^5(x+6)^2(x-3)^3</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Multiplicity</title>
            
        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            How do these different zeros show up on the graphs of the polynomial? They have to be points where the graph touches the <m>x</m>-axis, but is that all we know?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            How do these different zeros show up on the graphs of the polynomial? They have to be points where the graph touches the <m>x</m>-axis, but is that all we know? Show graphs, look at multiplicity, even and odd.
        </p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider the polynomial 
                    <md>
                        f(x) = (x+5)^3(x+2)^6(x-1)(x-4)^2
                    </md>.
                    Find all of the zeros and their muliplicity. Using the fact that the leading term of this polynomial is <m>1x^{12}</m>, sketch a graph of this function, including all of the zeros and how the graph interacts with them.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Factoring Polynomials</title>
            
<p component="b" workspace="2in">
    So, how do we get a polynomial into factored form? This is a bit trickier.
</p>
<p component="a">
    So, how do we get a polynomial into factored form? This is a bit trickier. There are a few ideas or strategies that can work, or we need to use a more detailed process that we will discuss in the next section.
</p>

<example workspace="3in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Find all of the zeros of <m>f(x) = x^3 - 7x^2 - 4x + 28</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>

</example>


        </paragraphs>
    </page>
<page>
    <example workspace="3in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find all of the zeros of <m>f(x) = x^4 - 2x^2 + 8</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>

    <example workspace="3in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find all of the zeros of <m>f(x) = x^4 - 5x^3 + 2x^2 - 10x</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>
    
</page>    

<page>
    <p component="b" workspace="2in">
        For zeros, we see that the real valued ones show up as intercepts on the graph, but the complex ones do not.
    </p>
    <p component="a">
        For zeros, we see that the real valued ones show up as intercepts on the graph, but the complex ones do not. When we're thinking in the conceptual context, we want to talk about zeros. From the practical point of view, it is usually the intercepts that matter more (but complex zeros do still have their applications).
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-polynomial-roots" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Every polynomial function of degree <m>n</m>, for <m>n \geq 1</m> has exactly <m>n</m> zeros (real or complex), when counted with multiplicity.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>
    
</page>

<page>
    <example workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Analyze the zeros and intercepts of the function 
                <md>
                    f(x) = x^5 + x^4 - 5x^3 - x^2 + 8x - 4 = (x-1)^3(x+2)^2
                </md>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    
    </example>
    
</page>

<page>
    <paragraphs>
        <title>Polynomial Models</title>
        
<p component="b" workspace="4in">
    Just like quadratics, we can use polynomials to modeal real-world situations. These problems often take two forms:
</p>
<p component="a">
    Just like quadratics, we can use polynomials to modeal real-world situations. These problems often take two forms: Given a polynomial and need to evaluate/analyze, or need to determine the polynomial from the set-up.
</p>

    </paragraphs>
</page>

<page>
    <example workspace="0in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                The polynomial function 
                <md>
                    M(t) = 0.5 t^4 + 3.45t^3 - 96.65t^2 + 347.7 t
                </md>
                can be used to estimate the number of milligrams of ibuprofen in the bloodstream <m>t</m> hours after a person takes a <m>400</m> mg dose.
                
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the number of milligrams of ibuprofen in the bloodstream at <m>t = 0,\ 0.5, 1, 1.5, </m> and so on, up to <m>t = 6</m> hr. Round values to the nearest tenth.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the domain, the relative maximum and where it occurs, and the range.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    
    </example>
    
</page>

</handout>

<handout component="cur">
    <title>Wednesday 11/4</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Use polynomial division to factor polynomials of degree at least 3.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Analyze the domain, vertical, and horizontal asymptotes of rational functions.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Polynomial Division and Factoring</title>

        <p component="b" workspace="2in">
            Last time, we mentioned how factoring polynomials was important for understanding how they work and what they do. If we can't factor by grouping or make it look like a quadratic, we need another approach.
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            Last time, we mentioned how factoring polynomials was important for understanding how they work and what they do. If we can't factor by grouping or make it look like a quadratic, we need another approach. When we're dividing numbers, we often find one factor and divide by it. That's what we're going to do here. 
        </p>

        <example workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use long division to find <m>1784 \div 9</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine if <m>(x+1)</m> and <m>(x+2)</m> are factors of <m>f(x) = x^3 - 3x^2 - 6x + 8</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Polynomial Division</title>
            
            <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                What is the general process of polynomial long division? If we want to divide <m>P(x)</m> by <m>d(x)</m>, what do we get?
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                What is the general process of polynomial long division? If we want to divide <m>P(x)</m> by <m>d(x)</m>, what do we get? Q and R, write out what it looks like and what that means for the degree.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>
    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compute <md>
                        (3x^3 - 7x + 4) \div (x+3)
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>
    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compute <md>
                        (2x^3 +5x^2  - 1) \div (x-2)
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>
    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For the polynomials <m>f(x) = 3x^3 - 7x + 4</m> and <m>g(x) = 2x^3 + 5x^2 - 1</m>, evaluate <m>f(-3)</m> and <m>g(2)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <p component="b" workspace="2in">
            What have we learned from this?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            What have we learned from this? <m>f(c) = 0</m> means <m>x-c</m> is a factor, then we can divide it out.
        </p>
    </page>
    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find all factors of <m>f(x) = x^3 + x^2 - 5x + 3</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find all factors of <m>f(x) = x^3 - x^2 - 10x - 8</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Rational Functions</title>

            <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                We've talked about rational expressions and functions before. What is the technical definition of these things?
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                We've talked about rational expressions and functions before. What is the technical definition of these things? Quotient of polynomials. Domain is where the denominator is zero.
            </p>
            
        </paragraphs>
    </page>
    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the domain and sketch the graph of the function 
                    <md>
                        f(x) = \frac{1}{x+4}
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>
    <page>
        <example workspace="0in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Find the domain of each of the following rational functions.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle f(x) = \frac{1}{x^2}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle g(x) = \frac{3x+1}{x-5}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle h(x) = \frac{x+1}{x^2 + x-6}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle r(x) = \frac{x^2 - 4x-5}{x^2 - 4x - 12}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Vertical Asymptotes</title>
            
        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            What happens to the graph of <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{x+4}</m> as the input value gets closer and closer to <m>-4</m>?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            What happens to the graph of <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{x+4}</m> as the input value gets closer and closer to <m>-4</m>? The outputs either get really big and positive, or really big and negative. Notate this with arrows to infinity. Left and right side notation.
        </p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

</handout>

<handout component="cur">
    <title>Friday 11/6</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Review of topics from the week.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Activity on polynomial division and domains.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Quiz on 3.4, 3.5, and 4.1.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Vertical Asymptotes</title>
            
        <p>
            This leads us to the definition of a vertical asymptote.
        </p>
        <definition xml:id="def-vertical-asymptote">
            <statement>
                <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                    Let <m>f(x)</m> be a rational function. We say that <m>x=a</m> is a vertical asymptote of <m>f(x)</m> if 
                </p>
                <p component="a">
                    Let <m>f(x)</m> be a rational function. We say that <m>x=a</m> is a vertical asymptote of <m>f(x)</m> if the values get really big (either positive or negative) as we approach <m>a</m> from at least one side.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <p component="b" workspace="2in">
            However, this isn't generally how we find these asymptotes:
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            However, this isn't generally how we find these asymptotes: Bottom is zero and top and bottom do not share any common factors.
        </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="0in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Find all of the vertical asymptotes for the following functions.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle f(x) = \frac{3x+5}{x^2 - 3x - 10}</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task workspace="4in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle g(x) = \frac{x^2 + 4x - 12}{x^3 - 7x^2 + 10x}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="0in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Find all of the vertical asymptotes for the following functions.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle f(x) = \frac{x^2 - x - 6}{x^2 +2x - 15}</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task workspace="4in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\displaystyle g(x) = \frac{x+4}{x^3 - 8x}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Review of the Week</title>

        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            What are the main things we discussed this week?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            What are the main things we discussed this week? Polynomials and how they can be used to solve problems, polynomial division to aid in factoring, rational functions and their domains and asymptotes.
        </p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>
</handout>

